import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"dsh analysis, Rho results and a selected-row question","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"records/dsh-rho-episode.md","filePath":"records/dsh-rho-episode.md"}'),n={name:"records/dsh-rho-episode.md"};function i(r,e,d,l,c,h){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Historical record</p><p>This page records what happened on a date, with its observed failures and limits. It is not a requirement for the current implementation. Current behaviour is in <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a>.</p></div><h1 id="dsh-analysis-rho-results-and-a-selected-row-question" tabindex="-1">dsh analysis, Rho results and a selected-row question <a class="header-anchor" href="#dsh-analysis-rho-results-and-a-selected-row-question" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;dsh analysis, Rho results and a selected-row question&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The current <strong>0.1.2</strong> bundle adds visible Harness entries for the complete Rho application; see <a href="#desktop-installation-and-visible-entry-points">Desktop installation</a>. The <a href="#reuse-the-whole-rho-application">earlier November 5 episode</a> records 0.1.0. The first episode below records the retired 0.0.1 embedded-view prototype, whose source remains in Git.</p><p>2026-10-31 local / 2026-11-01 UTC. This episode implements the requested <code>dsh-rho</code> prototype: analyse in dsh, show the outputs with existing Rho components, select a result and continue the original conversation. It follows the native plugin example in <a href="https://github.com/bowenliang123/dsh-context" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">dsh-context</a> and the installed dsh 0.2.1-rc.5 public APIs. The result is a local package, not a published npm or signed distribution.</p><h2 id="what-was-built" tabindex="-1">What was built <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-was-built" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What was built&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The application adapter lives in <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/blob/dfd305fbff03cbdae6a4d2296fd70028ff0ad1be/adapters/dsh-rho/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">the 0.0.1 adapter</a>. Its Cordis host registers <code>rho_present</code> and an authenticated result-reading route; its browser entry contributes <strong>Rho</strong> to dsh&#39;s <code>conversation.view</code> slot. Native session identity supplies both the tool&#39;s caller and the view&#39;s result scope. dsh owns analysis, permissions, tools, model and history. It is not started as a second Agent inside Rho.</p><p>The selected owners are Files 0.5.0, Tables 0.5.0, Media 0.4.0 and Contracts 0.4.0 from Rho-plugins commit <code>1173789010e4679180804aa309e661b218198f70</code>. Their public APIs, TableView and MediaView are consumed unchanged. The adapter supplies composition, session archives and a transient selection port; it reuses Rho&#39;s existing POSIX writer lease. No Core or Rho-plugins source changed, and neither the Core Host nor the Rho backend is started.</p><p><code>rho_present</code> captures completed project files. CSV columns and types are explicit; row selection needs a unique string key. Questions resolve those keys against the captured version, then use dsh&#39;s native revision-checked draft insertion and normal Send action. A question can be read or revised before sending. Closing the view, reading a result and reconnecting do not execute analysis.</p><h2 id="real-episode-and-observations" tabindex="-1">Real episode and observations <a class="header-anchor" href="#real-episode-and-observations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Real episode and observations&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The owned fixture used a disposable project and isolated <code>DSH_HOME</code>, dsh 0.2.1-rc.5, Node 22.22.3, Python 3 and headless Google Chrome on macOS. It used the existing configured gateway with <code>westlakehpc/deepseek-v4.1-flash</code>; credentials were read in memory and not put in source, patches or evidence. The person&#39;s running Desktop Harness and projects were not restarted.</p><p>The six input values were alpha: 1, 2, 8 and beta: -3, 4, 7. A single native prompt asked dsh to write and run a standard-library Python analysis once, append one run marker, emit a CSV and SVG, and present both with <code>rho_present</code>. The model wrote <code>analysis.py</code>, ran it and presented both outputs. The run took 3 minutes 32 seconds.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Observations</th><th>Mean in retained CSV</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>alpha</td><td>3</td><td>3.6666666666666665</td></tr><tr><td>beta</td><td>3</td><td>2.6666666666666665</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The browser showed both rows and the labelled two-bar figure. Selecting alpha changed the question label to <strong>Ask about 1 selected row</strong>. A Chinese question asked for its group, count and mean and whether this was the overall mean. The pre-existing draft <code>请用中文回答。</code> survived insertion. The added context contained only alpha, its exact numeric values, original path, snapshot digest, capture time and the same session ID. Sent once through the native composer, the model answered in 13 seconds: alpha, n = 3, mean approximately 3.67; this was alpha&#39;s mean and did not establish the combined mean. It ran no analysis for the question.</p><p>Retained identities:</p><ul><li>Session: <code>session-38a2a4c4-1e62-46f3-8ff6-234d7d098ca7</code>.</li><li>CSV tool call: <code>chatcmpl-tool-a6473a9aeace26b0</code>; SHA-256 <code>e2bba75703875eb8484e302157f29d28a42705fa3b21332897bf86f87ba31017</code>.</li><li>SVG tool call: <code>chatcmpl-tool-a9ee726ca0c532ff</code>; SHA-256 <code>1cb9430aed9b827880f63b866e539215b2daeab70926119ff4dfb22f9a027cb0</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="failures-that-changed-the-implementation" tabindex="-1">Failures that changed the implementation <a class="header-anchor" href="#failures-that-changed-the-implementation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Failures that changed the implementation&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>Loading an absolute package directory as a host module failed with an ESM directory import error. The source fixture now resolves the package by name; delivery uses an ordinary tarball installed by <code>dsh plugin</code>.</li><li>The first model session used dsh&#39;s retained default workspace instead of the disposable folder. It found no input and did no analysis. The fixture now explicitly creates/selects its workspace with dsh&#39;s public controller. The installation instructions require choosing the matching folder in dsh.</li><li>Comparing the browser Origin with the handler&#39;s internal request URL rejected valid result reads. dsh uses a carrier-neutral internal URL. The adapter now relies on the connection service&#39;s authenticated <code>/api</code> boundary and retains the cross-site refusal; it still validates original session and project scope.</li><li>Files&#39; captured resources name archive paths. Routing those paths back into the analysis project made four initial store checks fail. The adapter&#39;s path provider now resolves captured internal paths in its archive and ordinary paths through Files&#39; project containment. All five store checks then passed.</li><li>An exclusive lock file survived the first owned-host shutdown. Reusing Rho&#39;s kernel-held writer lease removed stale-unlink/PID-reuse recovery. A second writer is refused, and the installed plugin reopened the same archive after both normal shutdown and an abrupt kill of its owned dsh process.</li></ol><p>A browser assertion initially read an empty draft before asynchronous insertion completed. Waiting for the native Chat view switch resolved the observation; the same captured values were then verified. No analysis prompt was resent. Package assembly also caught nested AG Grid package metadata without adjacent licenses; notices now come from each actual bundled dependency root, including <code>ag-stack</code>. An installation-guide dry run also showed that <code>npm --prefix ... pack</code> selected the root application. The verified command now names <code>npm pack ./adapters/dsh-rho</code>.</p><h2 id="verification-and-installation" tabindex="-1">Verification and installation <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification-and-installation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification and installation&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>npm run build</code>, five store tests and a TypeScript check over the adapter&#39;s three source files passed. The tests cover snapshot overwrite/reopen, original-call retries, scope/path/key rejection, budgets and competing writers. They do not claim full Rho application acceptance; its unrelated suite was not run.</p><p>In the real browser:</p><ul><li>Table and SVG views, row selection, the existing Chinese draft and same-session model continuation all worked. Screens were inspected at 1440, 768 and 390 px; narrow results use scrolling. No browser page errors were recorded.</li><li>Offline result reads showed an error and <strong>Try again</strong> while keeping displayed results. Restoring the connection recovered those same results.</li><li>Editing the chat draft while a delayed question request was pending refused insertion and kept <code>Newer draft ✓</code> intact.</li><li>Refresh and a normal owned-host restart restored the original conversation and both results. The test then installed the tarball through <code>dsh plugin --profile web add &lt;tarball&gt; --ignore-scripts --offline</code>, which added and enabled its bundle. Its archive and draft insertion worked after launch.</li><li>Overwriting the original CSV with alpha mean 999 did not alter the displayed snapshot or the selected-row question, which still carried 3.6666666666666665. The test restored the original CSV afterward.</li><li>After an abrupt owned-host process kill, the installed package reopened the archive and original session without removing the lock file. At the end, <code>run-count.txt</code> still had exactly one line and the archive still had two results.</li></ul><p>Local evidence is under <code>work/dsh-rho/prototype-03/</code>: <code>evidence.json</code>, <code>chat.txt</code>, the native question drafts, original CSV, screenshots of empty/results/selection/ answer/failure/recovery/narrow states, and <code>dsh-rho-0.0.1.tgz</code>. The private launch address stays outside the repository. The fixture and native session history are retained in the disposable <code>/tmp/rho-dsh-prototype-03</code> directory. The package&#39;s composition manifest records exact bundle inputs and selected local overrides; it is not an official release attestation.</p><p>The delivered package is <code>work/dsh-rho/package/dsh-rho-0.0.1.tgz</code>, SHA-256 <code>4ffe8b2798d2d0ead6e558f9d26599bd63b1d5d0b7eecd54efa85684e5396266</code>. Final review keyed result choices and questions by original tool call as well as snapshot, so two presentations of identical bytes retain their own titles. The five tests and strict type check passed; the final tarball was installed again, and its table, figure, native draft insertion and original call ID were checked in the same session without resending analysis. The run count remained one.</p><h2 id="what-this-establishes-and-what-remains" tabindex="-1">What this establishes and what remains <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-this-establishes-and-what-remains" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What this establishes and what remains&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This observed episode establishes the requested local loop with reused components and one native dsh conversation. The adapter is deliberately one-project and one selected dsh release candidate. It has no general Studio, notebook, live variable inspection or automatic output discovery. Row selections are transient, image questions carry references rather than pixels, and a question&#39;s structured context is visible in the native draft. React comes from Harness; broader compatibility with the components&#39; declared React peer version is not established by this run.</p><p>The <a href="https://github.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/blob/dfd305fbff03cbdae6a4d2296fd70028ff0ad1be/adapters/dsh-rho/README.md#ownership-and-limits" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">0.0.1 package README</a> names the budgets, configuration and refusal behavior. Invalid table content can leave a bounded captured file unlisted. There is no silent deletion of earlier results or automatic replay of unknown work. Independent researcher use, Desktop/TUI entry points, Windows locking and public package distribution remain untested.</p><h2 id="reuse-the-whole-rho-application" tabindex="-1">Reuse the whole Rho application <a class="header-anchor" href="#reuse-the-whole-rho-application" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reuse the whole Rho application&quot;">​</a></h2><p>2026-11-05. The user asked to reuse the existing Rho at the lowest integration cost. Inspection of Harness 0.2.1-rc.5&#39;s installed MCP client and bundle reference, and its <a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/blob/master/packages/preset/agent-preset/skills/cordis-plugin-development/references/mcp-bundle.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">public bundle documentation</a>, showed that a bundle can contain configuration without Host or Client entry code. Rho already supplies the project grant, stdio MCP bridge, full WebUI and frozen review-context handoff. These existing boundaries were sufficient for the flow.</p><p>The 0.1.0 adapter now contains two configuration files, README and license. Its patch mounts Harness&#39;s existing MCP client and runs <code>rho --mcp</code> with an existing agent connection file. It adds no tool implementation, browser entry, service, result archive, Skill copy or model configuration. The previous 297 lines of runtime TypeScript, build script, lockfile and obsolete store tests were removed. No Core or component source changed. Rho&#39;s WebUI stays in its own window; its iframe policy was not relaxed. Unlike 0.0.1, returning a question requires one explicit copy/paste into the original Harness conversation.</p><h3 id="installed-application-episode" tabindex="-1">Installed application episode <a class="header-anchor" href="#installed-application-episode" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Installed application episode&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The disposable project was <code>/private/tmp/rho-dsh-reuse-20261105/projects/analysis</code>, with separate service state and <code>DSH_HOME</code>. The service used an already installed <code>@rho-workspace/rho@0.5.3</code> from <code>/tmp/rho-npm-053</code>, with its packaged components; no application build was needed. Source main was 0.5.4, but the registry returned 404 for that version at the time of this check. The person&#39;s other Rho service, Harness Desktop and projects were left running unchanged.</p><p>An owned Rho service granted this project to session <code>dsh</code>. The 0.1.0 tarball was installed through <code>dsh plugin --profile web add … --ignore-scripts --offline</code>. The unchanged <code>skills/rho</code> directory was linked into this trial&#39;s Harness skill folder. The model actually read it, resolved Rho operations, wrote a Python standard-library script, ran it through <code>kernel.run</code> and registered a keyed CSV and SVG through <code>resource.register</code>. Model credentials stayed in memory, outside the bundle and evidence.</p><p>The first configured model route, <code>westlakehpc/deepseek-v4.1-flash</code>, failed with an upstream duplicate-item-ID error after its initial tool calls. Both Chat Completions and Messages attempts failed, including a clean Messages session. The project still contained only the input file, and no Notebook cell had run. The trial then selected the already configured default route <code>fusecode-lab/claude-opus-5-5[1m]</code> through Messages. This succeeded. These are the configured route names, not independent verification of the model providers. The bundle makes no model selection and did not work around the failed gateway.</p><p>The successful analysis took 59 seconds in Harness. The six input values were again alpha: 1, 2, 8 and beta: -3, 4, 7. The script wrote:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>n</th><th>CSV mean</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>alpha</td><td>3</td><td>3.666667</td></tr><tr><td>beta</td><td>3</td><td>2.666667</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Notebook cell <code>k-441c12dcff1d7d0b</code>, change ID <code>analysis-run-20261105-1</code>, completed in the shared Python 3.13.1 session. Its output included both means, the CSV and <code>run-count lines: 1</code>. In the real browser, the Notebook showed the code, output and live <code>ns</code> value; existing table and media views displayed the two rows and the 400 × 300 SVG. Existing project notes were readable alongside the Studio table.</p><p>Selecting alpha and asking in Studio created question <code>q-99d55f47-8bcb-4f1b-bc57-b9bd9bcb7cc7</code>, with frozen context <code>1f4d589da930eaf21214d9024bfabedc7c5b76e1c7f655c674c1fd613269636d</code>. The retained CSV digest was <code>014d7d55f837f35a400dededec75999c5becd392a5355461be2795862509a4ca</code>. Through Review&#39;s <strong>External Agent · copy request</strong>, that context request was pasted and sent in the same Harness session. In 46 seconds the model read the context and wrote <code>annotation.reply</code>, retaining its original message identity and exact result versions. Rho displayed the Chinese answer: alpha, n = 3, mean 3.666667, the mean of that group rather than all six observations. The answer created a reply document; it did not run the analysis again.</p><p>The actual route needed <strong>Open in Studio</strong>: the ordinary file preview selected rows but did not show the question control. On this installed version, <strong>Review this question</strong> left the Studio visible, so the trial used the <strong>Review</strong> sidebar. Reading the clipboard before the asynchronous copied confirmation initially returned empty text; the disabled Harness Send button prevented an empty turn. Waiting for Rho&#39;s copied confirmation supplied the exact context request. The guide now follows these observed controls. No claim of one-click delivery is made.</p><h3 id="packaging-and-recovery-checks" tabindex="-1">Packaging and recovery checks <a class="header-anchor" href="#packaging-and-recovery-checks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Packaging and recovery checks&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The final package contains exactly <code>package.json</code>, <code>cordis.patch.yml</code>, <code>README.md</code> and <code>LICENSE</code>: 15,053 bytes compressed, 41,643 bytes unpacked. No dependencies or install/build scripts run. It was installed again with the ordinary offline bundle installer. A missing connection file then produced <code>ENOENT</code> and Harness&#39;s <code>rho-mcp … initial connection or tool synchronization failed</code> warning; Harness opened with the entry unavailable. The Plugins page showed installation, which by itself did not establish MCP readiness. Rho was not started or restarted by this failure.</p><p>While the owned Harness was stopped, the Rho Notebook remained idle with its original live value and one cell. Restoring the correct connection and restarting only Harness reopened the same conversation and allowed a passive read of the original cell. The run counter stayed one. The bundle&#39;s five-minute MCP reply wait is configurable; this episode did not exercise long-running cells, all four tools&#39; large-result paths, other operating systems, Desktop or TUI.</p><p>Local evidence is in <code>work/dsh-rho/reuse-01/</code>: chat and handoff text, the missing-file log, package inventory, screenshots of Notebook, table, figure, question and reply, and the observation summary. The private launch addresses and credentials remain in the disposable trial directory. The final tarball is <code>work/dsh-rho/package/dsh-rho-0.1.0.tgz</code>, SHA-256 <code>8b3b56f9d1c3370e8256ce167cece8753acd5a569e822e3036f4baadca64fd1e</code>.</p><p>The minimum entry route is an ordinary Harness bundle. Its <a href="https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/blob/master/packages/boot/plugin-market/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">market reader</a> uses user-configured indexes and has no default index in the tested release. Git subdirectory packages are supported by that installer, so a listing can point to an available source commit without packaging Rho again. Public Git installation, npm publication and market inclusion were not performed. The local package stays private. The old 0.0.1 source history and archive bytes remain; no migration or archive deletion was attempted. This is one observed integration episode, not full application acceptance or independent usability evidence.</p><h2 id="desktop-installation-and-visible-entry-points" tabindex="-1">Desktop installation and visible entry points <a class="header-anchor" href="#desktop-installation-and-visible-entry-points" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Desktop installation and visible entry points&quot;">​</a></h2><p>2026-11-05. The user installed the connection-only bundle and found no Rho tab or button in Harness. The missing entry was visible in their screenshot. Version 0.1.2 adds <strong>Rho</strong> to <code>conversation.view</code> and <code>sidebar.panellist</code>, both opening a small launcher. <strong>Open Rho workspace</strong> uses the existing <code>sidebarRight.openTab</code> Browser to display the complete application beside the conversation. <strong>Open in system browser</strong> remains available. The Rho application and its iframe policy are unchanged; Desktop uses Harness&#39;s native webview.</p><p>A small Host route reads at most 64 KiB from the chosen agent connection file, extracts its local service address and project ID, and returns only the project URL. Credential fields never leave the Host. It refuses remote or credential-bearing addresses, missing project scope and oversized files. The launcher neither starts a service nor submits analysis. The existing MCP entry and four tool names are unchanged. React comes from Harness; only the launcher is built, with esbuild 0.25.12. No Rho component code is bundled or modified.</p><p>The real installation used the person&#39;s already running Harness Desktop 0.2.1-rc.5 on macOS and the existing Rho 0.5.3 trial project <code>analysis</code>. Its original service, Notebook, one cell and retained results remained in place. Desktop refused <code>dsh plugin --profile desktop</code> because the Electron application exclusively owns that profile. Its <strong>Plugins → Add plugin → Enable now</strong> path installed the local tarball successfully. The prior profile files were backed up outside the repository. The unchanged Rho Skill was linked into Harness&#39;s skills directory.</p><p>An initial partial MCP config override failed schema validation: a profile <code>config</code> replaces the complete object, so command and arguments alone dropped <code>serverName</code> and <code>transport</code>. A complete override and a bundle off/on recovered it. Desktop&#39;s Finder launch did not use the shell exports, so its profile now explicitly names the same agent connection for the MCP and launcher entries. Neither the other configured MCP servers nor their grants were changed.</p><p>In the actual Desktop window, both Rho entries appeared and the final bundle reported <strong>2 running</strong> components. The conversation tab&#39;s open button and the sidebar&#39;s open button both displayed the full Rho project inside Harness&#39;s Browser. Switching back to Chat kept Rho beside it. The existing <code>summary.csv</code> displayed alpha (3, 3.667) and beta (3, 2.667), with alpha&#39;s prior selection retained. The final screen was visually inspected and left open for the person. This check opened existing work; it did not run a new model analysis or establish another selected-row model continuation beyond the earlier episode.</p><p>An isolated Web Harness exercised a missing launcher connection: the entry stayed visible, <strong>Open Rho workspace</strong> was disabled, and <strong>Try again</strong> was available. Restoring the file and retrying recovered the same launcher without restarting Rho. Another run found that a missing Browser provider switched away from the launcher before displaying its error. The final client checks the tab registry before navigation and restores the prior panel on a failed open. Repeating the same Web action then kept the launcher, its explanation and its system-browser link visible. Reusing the same local tarball version initially retained an older pnpm asset; checking actual installed bytes caught this, and the final 0.1.2 package uses a distinct version and tarball identity.</p><p>The adapter build and both credential-projection/error-route tests passed. The final installed Desktop files were compared to the source package. The tarball has six files and is 19,011 bytes compressed (51,727 unpacked), at <code>work/dsh-rho/package/dsh-rho-0.1.2.tgz</code>, SHA-256 <code>60dbeb0a5417d92682fa169f1fcb6d33e9efd6418bb4704b71bfe7a68c0d11cd</code>. Local evidence is under <code>work/dsh-rho/launcher-01/</code>, including the Desktop window, missing-connection and recovery screenshots and the installed-package summary. The owned Web check is disposable; the person&#39;s Desktop and existing Rho services stay available for their trial. Public distribution, TUI, other operating systems and independent usability evidence remain outside this check.</p>',52)])])}const b=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,b as default};
